WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.933
GraphRAG.

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:04.485
Ordinary retrieval has a blind spot.

3
00:00:04.485 --> 00:00:07.300
Here is what it is, and how a graph fixes it.

4
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.767
Ask: what are the main themes across five hundred reports?

5
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:17.700
Top-k retrieval returns the five chunks most similar to the word themes, and they answer nothing.

6
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.567
The answer is a property of the whole corpus, not of any chunk.

7
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:25.333
GraphRAG changes what gets indexed.

8
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:27.300
It starts with the whole corpus.

9
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.800
An LLM extracts entities and relations from every chunk, forming a knowledge graph.

10
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:37.767
A clustering algorithm called Leiden groups the graph into topical neighborhoods.

11
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:41.100
Then the LLM writes a summary for each community.

12
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.767
Here is the shape in code.

13
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:45.887
The offline build runs once.

14
00:00:45.887 --> 00:00:49.723
Extract entities, find communities, and summarize each one.

15
00:00:49.723 --> 00:00:51.933
The summaries are the costly step.

16
00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:59.767
At question time, we gather the relevant community summaries and synthesize one answer, map reduce style, not top k.

17
00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:02.300
Here is a real case.

18
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:07.833
A program manager asks which subsystems fail most often after long eclipse periods.

19
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:14.967
Top k returns five random mentions, because the connection lives across thirty reports that never cite each other.

20
00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:20.533
With a graph of subsystems, anomalies, and conditions, the community summaries can answer it.

21
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:24.400
The honest tradeoff: the build is expensive.

22
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.755
It makes an LLM call per chunk, plus summaries.

23
00:01:27.755 --> 00:01:31.200
Use it when your questions truly span many documents.

24
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.185
Let's recap.

25
00:01:33.185 --> 00:01:35.800
Chunk retrieval misses global questions.

26
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.633
GraphRAG builds a graph, clusters it, and summarizes each community.

27
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:43.700
Answers come from map reduce over those summaries.

28
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.267
The build is costly but done once.

29
00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:48.467
Next up: knowledge graphs in RAG.
